{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788384856,"nonce":"n8VLn2yOCaNX1t5d2Uk2MwvlmIM=","message":"Opened by Dorian in play, run openrun-20260902, day 6 Summer. This is a survey\nissue, not a single fix: **where else does the \"mod holds the loop\" pattern\napply?**\n\n\nEvery turn the agent spends asking \"is it done yet?\" costs an LLM call, a\nround trip, and a chunk of context — and produces no decision. The platform\nalready accepts this argument in several places and states it plainly:\n`turn.md §construction-stalled` says \"the mod holds the tick, so the mod holds\nthe transition\", and `§repeated-refusal` says \"the mod holds the count\nprecisely so the caller does not have to remember to derive it\". **The more\nmechanical and deterministic the mod is, the fewer calls the agent makes, and\nthe better the platform is.** This issue asks for that principle to be applied\nsystematically rather than case by case.\n\n\n572 protocol steps in ~6 in-game days with 4-5 colonists. The verb histogram:\n\n    95 things      60 pawn      47 journal    40 advance    30 digest\n    21 designate   18 zone      17 build      16 research   15 zones\n\n`things` at 95 and `pawn` at 60 are overwhelmingly POLLING, not deciding — the\nsame query re-issued to see whether a number moved yet. Concrete loops this run\nburned turns on, every one of them mechanical:\n\n- **Waiting for rice to become harvestable.** `zones` re-read repeatedly to\n  watch `growth_max_pct` crawl toward the 0.65 threshold. Wanted:\n  `advance --until.condition {path:\"zones.harvestable\", op:\"\u003e\", value:0}` or a\n  harvest-ready halt.\n- **Waiting for research.** `research` re-read to watch `progress/cost`.\n  Wanted: `advance --until.research \u003cproject\u003e`.\n- **Waiting for a ritual.** 5,000 ticks of bestowing ceremony with nothing to\n  decide (git-bug `9717e52` comment 3).\n- **Waiting for a pawn to arrive somewhere** — the taming trip, the 90-cell\n  mining trip, the walk back to food.\n- **Waiting for a build batch.** Partly solved: `--until.layout` exists and is\n  the model the rest should copy.\n- **Watching food fall.** Solved late by\n  `--until.condition {path:\"resources.food_days\"}`, which worked well and is\n  proof the mechanism is right.\n\n\n**A. Make every multi-tick, agent-initiated process waitable BY NAME.**\n`--until.layout ly-1` is the existing template. The `until` set is\n`{ticks|letter|threat|alert|event|condition|layout}` and `layout` is the only\ntransaction-scoped member. Candidates, roughly in value order:\n\n    research \u003cproject\u003e     bill \u003cuid\u003e completes      ritual / ceremony\n    quest \u003cid\u003e resolves    pawn \u003cid\u003e arrives at P    tame \u003cpawn\u003e resolves\n    caravan arrives/leaves temperature at target     designation batch clears\n    harvest ready in zone  trade caravan on map      prisoner recruited\n\nSafety halts (threat, casualty, red error) must still fire while waiting, the\nway they do under `--until.layout`.\n\n**B. Ask, for each standing rule and checklist item: should the MOD just do\nthis?** The ladder in `postmortem.md` already says a fully-computable response\nbelongs on the mod rung. Several standing rules are exactly that and are still\nbeing executed by hand every turn:\n\n- **Standing rule 2, \"butcher within one in-game day of any kill\".** Entirely\n  computable: the mod knows the kill tick, the corpse, the rot clock and\n  whether a butcher spot exists. This run had meat rot in storage anyway.\n- **Standing rule 4, \"query MinifiedThing every day\".** A count with a fixed\n  threshold — should be a digest trip-wire or an alert, not a daily manual\n  query.\n- **Standing rule 3, \"check every day for unworn armour\"** — same shape, and\n  `daily.md §armed-roster` already spells out the whole derivation.\n- **Re-sow after harvest**, **unforbid the battlefield after a raid**\n  (`cc8988c` covers the post-raid case), **haul finished goods out of a\n  workbench**.\n\nWhere the response is not fully computable, the honest answer is a trip-wire\nthat WAKES the agent with the decision already framed — which is what\n`triage` does today, and `triage` is the best thing on the surface: it returns\nthe bleed clock, every candidate rescuer with the game's own refusal reason, a\nverdict, AND `act` as a ready-to-send envelope. **That is the shape to aim at\neverywhere:** the mod does the arithmetic and hands the agent a decision, not\na data dump.\n\n\n- A written audit, in the repo, listing every multi-tick agent-initiated\n  process and every standing rule, each marked: `until-matcher` /\n  `mod-side procedure` / `stays with the agent (a real judgement call)` — with\n  one line of reasoning each.\n- The `until` matchers from list A that the audit marks worth doing are filed\n  as their own issues with acceptance, rather than being implemented ad hoc.\n- A measurement to check the work landed: re-run a comparable session and\n  compare protocol steps per in-game day against this run's baseline of\n  **572 steps / ~6 days = ~95 steps per in-game day**. That number is the\n  point of the exercise and should fall.","files":null}]}